course: biology
lesson: 33
title: Birds: feathers, wings, eggs and flight
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Birds are the only animals alive today with feathers, and nearly every part of a bird is built to be light and strong for flying.
explain: Put a pigeon feather under a magnifying glass: a shaft in the middle, hundreds of branches called barbs, and on them tiny barbules with hooks that zip together like Velcro.
explain: Snap a chicken bone from Sunday dinner and it is hollow, with thin struts inside like a bridge, light but strong.
explain: Birds are living dinosaurs: a chicken's scaly foot with three forward toes looks just like a dinosaur's in the museum because it was inherited from them.
explain: They have no teeth, so a hen swallows little pebbles that grind food in a muscly gizzard, and that white splat on the car is not poo at all but the bird's version of wee.
fact: A feather has a central shaft with barbs, and each barb has tiny barbules with hooks that zip together like Velcro; birds re-zip them by preening.
fact: Many bird bones are hollow, with thin crossing struts, and some are filled with air connected to the lungs.
fact: Birds make no runny urine: their waste comes out as a white paste of uric acid, together with dark poo, from the same opening.
fact: An egg has an air pocket at the wide end, white for water and cushioning, a yolk for food, and thousands of tiny holes in the shell that let air in; before it breaks the shell the chick takes its first breaths from the air pocket.
fact: Supermarket eggs never hatch because the hens live without a cockerel, and a hen lays about one egg a day.
fact: A chick breaks out using an egg tooth, a hard point on its beak that falls off a few days after hatching; people should not help it out, because it is still taking in the last of its yolk and will finish by itself.
fact: Birds evolved from small feathered meat-eating dinosaurs, the same group as T. rex, so scientists count birds as living dinosaurs.
fact: Ducks spread oil from a gland near the tail over their tightly zipped feathers, so rain runs off them; thick oil from a spill at sea glues the feathers so water gets in and the bird gets soaked and cold.
fact: Geese fly in a V because each bird rides the rising air from the wingtip of the bird in front, saving energy; they take turns at the front.
fact: Swallows from Britain fly about 10,000 km to South Africa for the winter, because a British winter has almost no flying insects for them to eat, and South Africa has plenty.
fact: When a perching bird bends its legs, tendons pull its toes tight round the branch, so it can sleep without falling off.
fact: Birds have no teeth; swallowed grit in a muscly stomach called the gizzard grinds the food, and having no heavy jaws full of teeth keeps the head light for flying.
fact: Most birdsong is sung by males to claim a territory, but European robins of both sexes sing in winter to defend their patch; birds sing loudest at dawn, when the air is still and quiet and songs carry far.
exercise: What holds the branches of a feather together? | Tiny hooks on the barbules that zip like Velcro | A thin layer of sticky glue that the bird spits on them | The wind pressing them flat | Small muscles in each branch
exercise: What is a bird doing when it runs its feathers through its beak? | Preening, zipping the feathers back together | Eating tiny insects from its skin | Pulling out all its old feathers | Sharpening its beak on the hard feather shafts
exercise: You snap a chicken bone in half and find it is hollow. Why are bird bones like that? | Hollow bones with struts are light but strong, good for flying | The chicken was ill and its bones slowly wasted away inside it | Cooking melted the inside of the bone | All animal bones are hollow like straws
exercise: What is the white part of bird droppings on a windscreen? | A paste of uric acid, the bird's version of wee | Chalk and sand that the bird swallowed by mistake with its food | Milk the bird has drunk | Dried feathers mixed with poo
exercise: What is the yolk of an egg for? | Food for the growing chick | Air for the chick to breathe | A soft cushion against bumps | The chick's first soft feathers
exercise: Why do eggs from the supermarket never hatch? | The hens that lay them live without a cockerel | They are kept far too cold in the shop and on the lorry | The shop removes the chicks first | The yolk is taken out at the farm
exercise: How does a chick get out of its egg? | It chips the shell with an egg tooth on its beak | Its mother cracks the egg open for it with her beak | The shell slowly melts away when the chick is ready | It kicks the shell open with its strong little feet
exercise: Why does a chicken's foot look like a dinosaur's foot? | Birds evolved from dinosaurs and inherited their feet | Chickens copy the shape of dinosaurs' feet to scare foxes away | It is just a funny coincidence | Dinosaurs evolved from chickens
exercise: Why does rain run off a duck like beads of glass? | Oil from a gland and tightly zipped feathers keep water out | Ducks have a thin plastic-like skin under the feathers to keep dry | Ducks are always warmer than the rain | Ducks shake every drop off at once
exercise: Why do geese fly in a V shape? | Each rides rising air from the bird in front, saving energy | So that they can all see the leader's face and follow where it goes | It is the only shape their wings allow | To scare away eagles with a big arrow
exercise: Why do swallows leave Britain in September? | There are no flying insects to eat there in winter | They are chased away by robins | British winters are too cold and wet for them to build their nests | They go to Africa to lay their eggs
exercise: About how far do British swallows fly to their winter home? | About 10,000 km, to South Africa | About 100 km, just across to France | About 1,000 km, to Spain or Portugal | They do not travel anywhere at all
exercise: Why doesn't a sparrow fall off its branch when it sleeps? | Bending its legs pulls tendons that lock its toes round the branch | It ties itself firmly to the branch with a sticky thread of its own spit | It stays awake and holds on all night | It sleeps standing on one wing
exercise: Why does a hen swallow small pebbles in the farmyard? | Grit in her gizzard grinds food, since birds have no teeth | To make herself heavier in the wind | By mistake, because she cannot see very well what she is pecking | To make the shells of her eggs
exercise: Why does a robin sing from a fence on a cold winter morning? | To defend its feeding territory | Because it is so happy to see snow | To call its babies home to the nest | To warm up its beak and its throat
exercise: Who sings the dawn chorus in spring, mostly? | Male birds claiming a territory | Baby birds asking for food | Female birds calling for help | Only owls and nightingales
test: Your little brother finds a chick halfway out of its egg and wants to pull the shell off. What should you say? | Leave it, the chick is still using up its yolk and will do it itself | Yes, help it, chicks cannot get out alone and will die without you | Pour warm water on the shell | Shake the egg so it comes out faster
test: In a cartoon a goose flies alone at the front for the whole trip. What really happens? | The geese take turns at the tiring front place | The biggest goose always leads | The youngest goose always leads | Geese never fly in groups at all, each one travels on its own
test: my sister says the white bit of bird poo is calcium from eggshells. is it? | No, it is uric acid, the bird's version of wee | Yes, birds poo out spare eggshell | Yes, but only hens that lay eggs every day have it in their poo | No, it is milk
test: What would happen to a duck covered in oil from a spill at sea? | Its feathers would let water in and it would get soaked and cold | It would float even better | Nothing at all, as ducks are already oily, so a bit more does not matter | It would fly faster
test: If birds had heavy jaws full of teeth like crocodiles, what problem would they have? | Their heads would be heavy, making flight harder | They could not sing | They would have to live underwater | Nothing, teeth weigh nothing at all compared with the rest of the head
test: A goose flies a little behind and to the side of the one in front. Why there? | That is where swirling air from the wingtip gives a lift | So it can talk to the goose in front | It is the warmest spot in the flock | So that it can steal bits of food from the leader's beak as they fly
test: shut it, birds aint dinosaurs, dinosaurs are all dead. who's right? | Birds evolved from dinosaurs, so scientists call them living dinosaurs | Dinosaurs evolved from birds | Birds are a kind of mammal | Birds and dinosaurs are not related at all, they just look a bit alike
test: What is the air pocket at the wide end of an egg for? | The chick's first breath before it breaks the shell | Keeping the egg from sinking in the nest | Storing the chick's food and water for later, until it is ready to hatch | Nothing, it is just empty
test: In a game, birds have solid heavy bones to make them tough. Why would real flying birds be worse off? | Heavy bones would make flying cost far more energy | Solid bones would break much more easily in the air | Birds would grow too tall | They would not be able to see
test: A barn swallow ringed in a Devon barn is found in South Africa in winter. What does this show? | Swallows migrate thousands of kilometres for winter | Someone carried it there by plane | It was blown off course in a big storm | Swallows live in Africa all year
test: Why do birds sing loudest at dawn? | The air is still and quiet, so songs carry far | They are complaining loudly that they are hungry after the long night | Birds can only see at dawn | The sun makes their throats warm
exam: Identify the principal function of the hooked barbules of a feather. | To interlock the barbs into a continuous surface | To anchor the feather within the skin | To store oil for waterproofing | To sense vibrations of the air during flight
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the formation of geese in flight? | Each bird benefits from rising air generated by the wingtip of the bird ahead | The formation serves solely for display | The leading bird bears no additional burden | The formation is maintained by vocal command alone, issued by the eldest goose of the flock
exam: Ascertain the role of grit retained within the avian gizzard. | The mechanical grinding of food | The production of eggshell material | The ballasting of the bird in flight | The neutralisation of stomach acid
exam: It is given that eggs are produced by hens maintained without a cockerel. Determine the consequence. | The eggs are incapable of hatching | The eggs hatch more rapidly | The eggs yield only male chicks | The eggs possess no yolk
